{
 "test": "Study areas are Manhattan tax blocks, chosen at random, with the explorer's own options per parcel (zoning, protections, precedent). Every plan of a block (every combination of options) is enumerated, so the true front is known: * small blocks (64-2,000 plans): every plan is also scored by the full impact engine, so the exact front is known,   and the explorer's front (re-scored exactly, as in the Studio) is compared with it; this measures search and   first-order (surrogate) error together; * medium blocks (20,000-1,000,000 plans): the front of the first-order pillar gains the explorer searches is known;   this measures the search alone. The exhaustive surrogate front is also re-scored exactly (up to 300 plans) and   compared with the explorer's re-scored front. Quality is the hypervolume of a front as a share of the hypervolume of the true front (pillar values normalised to the range of the plans compared; the worst value of each pillar is the reference point), and the share of true-front plans the explorer returns. A random search with the same number of evaluations as NSGA-III (population 92 x 120 generations) is the baseline.",
